summary:
The Stim Optimizer page gives honest answers, and the 2026-09-26 design review cut a lot, but it still asks too much of the reader. It answers two questions: should today's setting change, and can closed loop start. Those answers are spread over five headlines on five separate cards. The closed-loop question is split between the first card (sensing pairs) and the last (the four checks), with the open-loop decision in between. Most of the reading effort goes on ALL-CAPS 11 px column headers, tables of seven to nine columns, and about a dozen folds. On top of that, the page uses at least five slightly different ambers, reds and greens for three meanings. The status line's yellow key says "not evaluated", but it also marks things that were evaluated (a coverage shortfall, a pain map that moves over time). The changes that would matter most are these:
- merge the page into three sections (today's setting, closed loop, next visit), each opened by one answer;
- replace the wide tables with short aligned comparisons and plain sentences;
- draw the current maps as small multiples with one shared key and labels on the plot instead of a 140-word legend;
- keep the safe current ceiling in view at the top;
- reduce the colours to one set of tokens with a single meaning each.
Several of these changes touch jest tests that pin page text or card order; each finding says where.
